# /etc/x6100/remote.conf - the remote Icoms x6100_trx can be a control head for
#
# Mode 600 (it holds passwords in plain text): chmod 600 /etc/x6100/remote.conf.
# Readable by others and x6100_trx leaves the whole file unused (with a warning).
# Never put your own copy in a repository; build.sh backup keeps it in
# ~/.config/x6100 at mode 600, x6100_giveaway.sh removes it.
#
# One rig a line, at most 16:
#   <name> <host> <udp port> <user> <password> [txdummy] [txdgain=N]
#
#   name       shown on the RIG panel and used by `rig <name>` / `--remote <name>`;
#              no spaces, at most 23 characters, not LOCAL (the X6100 itself)
#   host       name or IP address of the rig (its LAN port, or wfserver)
#   udp port   the control port, 50001 on an Icom
#   user       a user of the rig's network settings (or of wfserver)
#   password   its password, no spaces
#   txdummy    transmit too: the X6100 keys itself at 0.1 W into a DUMMY LOAD on its
#              antenna port, its mic goes to the rig as LAN audio (set the rig's
#              DATA OFF MOD to LAN). Without it: receive only
#   txdgain=N  the TX audio level to this rig in %, 0-400 (default 3: an IC-7851 with
#              its LAN input at 50 %)
#
# Switch on the radio: hold GEN (status page at 0.6 s, the RIG panel at 1.5 s), MFK
# turn chooses, push switches. Console: `rig` lists, `rig <name>` / `rig local`
# switches. x6100_trx starts on the rig (or LOCAL) it was left on.
#
# Placeholders below: replace them, or start from an empty file.

#IC7851   rig.example.org   50001   operator   secret   txdummy txdgain=3
#IC705    192.168.1.50      50001   operator   secret
